1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,946
Training a tiny adapter can exhaust your GPU.

2
00:00:02,946 --> 00:00:05,061
That is the first distinction to make before

3
00:00:05,061 --> 00:00:07,862
fine-tuning a local model. Three point six

4
00:00:07,862 --> 00:00:10,771
nine million trainable parameters for query and

5
00:00:10,771 --> 00:00:13,475
value alone, or twenty-nine point nine three

6
00:00:13,475 --> 00:00:15,940
million when all seven projections are targeted.

7
00:00:16,262 --> 00:00:18,245
The adapter file describes the changes you

8
00:00:18,245 --> 00:00:20,469
learned. It does not describe everything the

9
00:00:20,469 --> 00:00:22,693
machine needed while learning them. Loading a

10
00:00:22,693 --> 00:00:24,928
model and answering a question proves that

11
00:00:24,928 --> 00:00:27,452
an inference workload fits. Training that same

12
00:00:27,452 --> 00:00:29,290
model creates a different memory bill.

13
00:00:29,620 --> 00:00:31,988
Three separate problems, three separate parts of the

14
00:00:31,988 --> 00:00:33,983
machine, and every one of them is on

15
00:00:33,983 --> 00:00:36,495
this screen right now. Quantise it and the

16
00:00:36,495 --> 00:00:39,211
file shrinks. Load it into memory. And keep

17
00:00:39,211 --> 00:00:40,305
it on a drive that does not

18
00:00:40,305 --> 00:00:42,840
spin. Count them as three separate budgets.

19
00:00:43,160 --> 00:00:45,174
Training requires far more than the saved

20
00:00:45,174 --> 00:00:48,011
adapter. The base model stays frozen, but

21
00:00:48,011 --> 00:00:51,220
every forward pass stores temporary activations, and

22
00:00:51,220 --> 00:00:55,006
backpropagation calculates gradients for the trainable matrices.

23
00:00:55,006 --> 00:00:57,120
That execution state fills your memory.

24
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:00,261
LoRA freezes the original weight matrix and learns

25
00:01:00,261 --> 00:01:03,566
an additional update through two smaller matrices.

26
00:01:03,566 --> 00:01:04,936
Think of the frozen model as a

27
00:01:04,936 --> 00:01:07,485
machine whose controls you are adjusting. You

28
00:01:07,485 --> 00:01:09,864
do not rebuild every component. But the

29
00:01:09,864 --> 00:01:12,530
machine still runs during each training example.

30
00:01:12,850 --> 00:01:15,226
The error signal still travels backward through the

31
00:01:15,226 --> 00:01:18,291
computation so the adapter can learn. Intermediate

32
00:01:18,291 --> 00:01:20,779
tensors needed for that backward pass remain

33
00:01:20,779 --> 00:01:22,810
part of the job. Frozen weights are

34
00:01:22,810 --> 00:01:25,110
not permission to delete the learning process.

35
00:01:25,430 --> 00:01:27,922
The adapter arithmetic is concrete. For a

36
00:01:27,922 --> 00:01:30,771
projection with input width two thousand forty-eight

37
00:01:30,771 --> 00:01:33,379
and output width two thousand forty-eight, rank

38
00:01:33,379 --> 00:01:35,614
sixteen times their sum gives one hundred

39
00:01:35,614 --> 00:01:38,198
two thousand four hundred added values. One

40
00:01:38,198 --> 00:01:40,602
matrix maps the input into the rank,

41
00:01:40,602 --> 00:01:42,490
another maps the rank into the output.

42
00:01:42,810 --> 00:01:45,653
Converting it into a memory prediction requires another

43
00:01:45,653 --> 00:01:49,571
question: what precision holds those values, their gradients,

44
00:01:49,571 --> 00:01:51,855
and the optimizer history? Then we still have

45
00:01:51,855 --> 00:01:53,260
to account for the rest of training.

46
00:01:53,580 --> 00:01:56,049
Use an actual architecture. Qwen two point

47
00:01:56,049 --> 00:01:59,547
five, three billion, Instruct has thirty-six layers

48
00:01:59,547 --> 00:02:01,229
and a hidden width of two thousand

49
00:02:01,229 --> 00:02:03,934
forty-eight. Its sixteen query heads share

50
00:02:03,934 --> 00:02:06,020
only two key and value heads.

51
00:02:06,340 --> 00:02:08,808
Each attention head is one hundred twenty-eight values

52
00:02:08,808 --> 00:02:12,065
wide. Two key and value heads serve sixteen

53
00:02:12,065 --> 00:02:14,913
query heads, so the value projection produces two

54
00:02:14,913 --> 00:02:18,351
hundred fifty-six outputs, not two thousand forty-eight. The

55
00:02:18,351 --> 00:02:20,860
narrow projections are the smaller ones to train.

56
00:02:21,180 --> 00:02:23,507
Put rank sixteen adapters on query and

57
00:02:23,507 --> 00:02:26,801
value only. The query projection adds sixty-five

58
00:02:26,801 --> 00:02:29,685
thousand five hundred thirty-six values per layer

59
00:02:29,685 --> 00:02:32,173
and the value projection thirty-six thousand eight

60
00:02:32,173 --> 00:02:34,630
hundred sixty-four. That is the whole adapter.

61
00:02:34,950 --> 00:02:37,585
Multiply by thirty-six layers and the total

62
00:02:37,585 --> 00:02:41,047
is three million, six hundred eighty-six thousand,

63
00:02:41,047 --> 00:02:44,172
four hundred parameters, with no trained biases.

64
00:02:44,172 --> 00:02:46,170
That count is derived, not measured.

65
00:02:46,490 --> 00:02:49,367
Expand that same rank to query, key,

66
00:02:49,367 --> 00:02:52,288
value, attention output, and the three feed-forward

67
00:02:52,288 --> 00:02:55,918
projections. The exact total is twenty-nine million,

68
00:02:55,918 --> 00:02:58,946
nine hundred thirty-three thousand, five hundred sixty-eight

69
00:02:58,946 --> 00:03:01,790
trainable parameters. The rank stays at sixteen.

70
00:03:02,110 --> 00:03:04,316
The base architecture did not change, and

71
00:03:04,316 --> 00:03:05,891
the places you allowed it to adapt

72
00:03:05,891 --> 00:03:08,150
changed. That is the whole difference between

73
00:03:08,150 --> 00:03:09,650
the two counts you just computed.

74
00:03:09,970 --> 00:03:11,503
That is why somebody else saying rank

75
00:03:11,503 --> 00:03:15,398
sixteen is insufficient information. Ask which projections,

76
00:03:15,398 --> 00:03:17,898
which layers, whether embeddings or biases are

77
00:03:17,898 --> 00:03:20,866
trained, and what precision the implementation actually

78
00:03:20,866 --> 00:03:23,474
uses. A small rank is a setting.

79
00:03:23,474 --> 00:03:25,490
It is not a complete training specification.

80
00:03:25,810 --> 00:03:28,953
On GPT three with one hundred seventy-five billion

81
00:03:28,953 --> 00:03:32,450
parameters, full fine-tuning scored seventy-three point eight

82
00:03:32,450 --> 00:03:35,203
on Wiki SQL and eighty-nine point five

83
00:03:35,203 --> 00:03:37,005
on matched Multi N L I. A

84
00:03:37,005 --> 00:03:38,965
LoRA row with four point seven million

85
00:03:38,965 --> 00:03:41,561
scored seventy-three point four and ninety-one point

86
00:03:41,561 --> 00:03:43,740
seven. Those are the paper's own numbers.

87
00:03:44,060 --> 00:03:46,645
Those are the original authors' task results,

88
00:03:46,645 --> 00:03:49,734
with task-specific variation, not measurements from this

89
00:03:49,734 --> 00:03:52,246
channel or promises for a desktop model.

90
00:03:52,246 --> 00:03:54,734
Now separate LoRA from quantized LoRA, because

91
00:03:54,734 --> 00:03:56,010
they are not the same method.

92
00:03:56,330 --> 00:03:59,630
Quantized LoRA keeps the small trainable matrices and

93
00:03:59,630 --> 00:04:02,154
changes how the frozen base is stored. The

94
00:04:02,154 --> 00:04:04,414
base sits in a reduced precision, and

95
00:04:04,414 --> 00:04:06,469
the adapters still train in a higher

96
00:04:06,469 --> 00:04:09,110
one. One method, two different memory bills.

97
00:04:09,430 --> 00:04:12,180
In the final QLoRA paper, a Llama seven

98
00:04:12,180 --> 00:04:15,293
billion model tuned on Alpaca scored thirty-eight point

99
00:04:15,293 --> 00:04:17,697
four on five-shot M M L U using

100
00:04:17,697 --> 00:04:21,183
brain float sixteen adapter tuning. Normal float four

101
00:04:21,183 --> 00:04:24,110
with double quantization scored thirty-nine point zero.

102
00:04:24,430 --> 00:04:26,861
On F L A N version two, the

103
00:04:26,861 --> 00:04:29,818
corresponding scores were forty-five point six for brain

104
00:04:29,818 --> 00:04:32,426
float sixteen and forty-four point five for

105
00:04:32,426 --> 00:04:34,998
normal float four. One dataset moved up

106
00:04:34,998 --> 00:04:37,198
zero point six points, the other down

107
00:04:37,198 --> 00:04:39,469
one point one. It has a boundary:

108
00:04:39,469 --> 00:04:43,020
the named model, datasets, methods, and evaluation.

109
00:04:43,340 --> 00:04:46,047
It does not establish that four-bit training improves

110
00:04:46,047 --> 00:04:48,331
every task. It does not tell us whether

111
00:04:48,331 --> 00:04:50,747
our Qwen adapter follows an internal coding

112
00:04:50,747 --> 00:04:53,060
convention, or whether your data follows one.

113
00:04:53,392 --> 00:04:55,435
For that decision, write down the behavior you

114
00:04:55,435 --> 00:04:57,562
want to change and examples that test it

115
00:04:57,562 --> 00:05:00,579
before training begins. The only number guaranteed to

116
00:05:00,579 --> 00:05:03,040
improve is the number of checkpoints on disk.

117
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:07,309
The famous sixty-five-billion model on a forty-eight-gigabyte GPU

118
00:05:07,309 --> 00:05:10,217
is also a scoped research demonstration. Keep the

119
00:05:10,217 --> 00:05:12,886
original recipe attached to that sentence. It

120
00:05:12,886 --> 00:05:14,989
is not a universal allowance for longer

121
00:05:14,989 --> 00:05:18,030
examples, a different architecture, or arbitrary batch

122
00:05:18,030 --> 00:05:20,440
sizes. The scope travels with the number.

123
00:05:20,760 --> 00:05:23,159
Treat it as evidence that quantized adapter

124
00:05:23,159 --> 00:05:26,224
training can make a previously inaccessible experiment

125
00:05:26,224 --> 00:05:29,096
possible. Then budget your own experiment. An

126
00:05:29,096 --> 00:05:32,233
engineering result becomes misleading when the equipment

127
00:05:32,233 --> 00:05:34,880
and settings disappear while the headline survives.

128
00:05:35,250 --> 00:05:37,918
Four-bit training does not mean every training tensor

129
00:05:37,918 --> 00:05:40,779
uses four bits. In the documented bits and

130
00:05:40,779 --> 00:05:43,808
bytes route, normal float four stores the converted

131
00:05:43,808 --> 00:05:46,104
base weights, and a separate compute setting

132
00:05:46,104 --> 00:05:49,577
selects the arithmetic precision. Our configuration uses

133
00:05:49,577 --> 00:05:52,960
brain float sixteen computation on compatible hardware.

134
00:05:53,280 --> 00:05:55,972
The adapters are extra trainable tensors on top

135
00:05:55,972 --> 00:05:59,951
of the frozen base. Activations, gradients, and optimizer

136
00:05:59,951 --> 00:06:02,823
states have their own representations. Read those

137
00:06:02,823 --> 00:06:04,987
settings as separate lines in a budget,

138
00:06:04,987 --> 00:06:06,660
and name the precision for each one.

139
00:06:06,980 --> 00:06:10,214
Multiplying the model's advertised parameter count by half

140
00:06:10,214 --> 00:06:12,702
a byte does not produce the peak. Even

141
00:06:12,702 --> 00:06:14,974
the converted weights need their scales: a

142
00:06:14,974 --> 00:06:17,102
scale stored in thirty-two bits for each

143
00:06:17,102 --> 00:06:19,602
block of sixty-four weights adds half a

144
00:06:19,602 --> 00:06:21,356
bit per weight. That is four and

145
00:06:21,356 --> 00:06:23,610
a half bits, before the scales shrink.

146
00:06:23,930 --> 00:06:27,322
Nested quantization compresses the scales as well, taking

147
00:06:27,322 --> 00:06:29,617
back zero point three seven three of a

148
00:06:29,617 --> 00:06:32,430
bit. The large-tensor average becomes four point

149
00:06:32,430 --> 00:06:34,569
one two seven bits per weight. It

150
00:06:34,569 --> 00:06:36,420
is larger than four bits, and it

151
00:06:36,420 --> 00:06:38,415
says nothing about tensors the loader did

152
00:06:38,415 --> 00:06:40,520
not convert. Read it as an average.

153
00:06:40,850 --> 00:06:42,713
Here is a quieter trap. The pinned

154
00:06:42,713 --> 00:06:45,297
P E F T preparation function freezes

155
00:06:45,297 --> 00:06:48,663
the base and promotes ordinary half-precision parameters

156
00:06:48,663 --> 00:06:51,451
to full precision, excluding the special four-bit

157
00:06:51,451 --> 00:06:54,300
parameters. That promotion is a full precision

158
00:06:54,300 --> 00:06:56,370
component you cannot see from the label.

159
00:06:56,690 --> 00:06:58,825
Our Qwen model has a tied vocabulary

160
00:06:58,825 --> 00:07:01,529
embedding. That embedding contains about three hundred

161
00:07:01,529 --> 00:07:04,017
eleven million values, and at four bytes

162
00:07:04,017 --> 00:07:05,977
each it is about one point two

163
00:07:05,977 --> 00:07:09,246
four five decimal gigabytes. This is derived

164
00:07:09,246 --> 00:07:11,970
tensor arithmetic, not an allocation measurement.

165
00:07:12,328 --> 00:07:15,453
Placement is another separate decision. The pinned Transformers

166
00:07:15,453 --> 00:07:19,035
guide says automatic device mapping is for inference.

167
00:07:19,035 --> 00:07:21,054
It is not evidence that an inference offload

168
00:07:21,054 --> 00:07:24,263
layout is a valid training layout. Our inspection

169
00:07:24,263 --> 00:07:27,653
plan names one CUDA device explicitly and disables

170
00:07:27,653 --> 00:07:30,880
the inference cache. Neither choice manufactures memory.

171
00:07:31,200 --> 00:07:34,101
Disabling the cache removes a mechanism for reusing

172
00:07:34,101 --> 00:07:37,046
past keys and values during generation. It does

173
00:07:37,046 --> 00:07:39,618
not remove the tensors needed to differentiate

174
00:07:39,618 --> 00:07:42,118
a training example. A successful loader is

175
00:07:42,118 --> 00:07:44,534
still only the beginning of the experiment,

176
00:07:44,534 --> 00:07:46,710
and the experiment is the expensive part.

177
00:07:47,031 --> 00:07:49,255
The useful controls change different parts of

178
00:07:49,255 --> 00:07:52,764
that experiment. Gradient checkpointing saves fewer intermediate

179
00:07:52,764 --> 00:07:56,009
tensors and recomputes them during backward, trading

180
00:07:56,009 --> 00:07:58,642
additional work for memory. A microbatch of

181
00:07:58,642 --> 00:08:00,970
one keeps fewer examples live together.

182
00:08:01,291 --> 00:08:04,644
Accumulating eight such microsteps builds an effective batch

183
00:08:04,644 --> 00:08:07,853
of eight on one device, without processing all

184
00:08:07,853 --> 00:08:11,099
eight simultaneously. Sequence length remains its own

185
00:08:11,099 --> 00:08:13,839
control, and these choices do not guarantee

186
00:08:13,839 --> 00:08:17,140
the same optimization behavior in every implementation.

187
00:08:17,460 --> 00:08:20,156
Return to the twenty-nine point nine three million

188
00:08:20,156 --> 00:08:23,834
parameter adapter. Assume full-precision adapter weights at four

189
00:08:23,834 --> 00:08:27,283
bytes each, full-precision gradients at four bytes each,

190
00:08:27,283 --> 00:08:30,120
and two full-precision Adam moment buffers, eight bytes

191
00:08:30,120 --> 00:08:33,060
together. That is sixteen bytes per trainable value.

192
00:08:33,380 --> 00:08:36,091
Our exact count is twenty-nine million, nine

193
00:08:36,091 --> 00:08:39,385
hundred thirty-three thousand, five hundred sixty-eight trainable

194
00:08:39,385 --> 00:08:42,642
values. Sixteen bytes per value yields four

195
00:08:42,642 --> 00:08:45,406
hundred fifty-six point seven five mebibytes for

196
00:08:45,406 --> 00:08:47,690
those four arrays. That is a theoretical

197
00:08:47,690 --> 00:08:50,620
adapter subtotal, not your full training footprint.

198
00:08:50,940 --> 00:08:53,853
That subtotal covers the adapter arrays only. The

199
00:08:53,853 --> 00:08:57,050
frozen base, the saved activations, and the temporary

200
00:08:57,050 --> 00:09:00,115
workspaces all sit outside it, and so does

201
00:09:00,115 --> 00:09:02,976
anything else the implementation keeps. Calling it the

202
00:09:02,976 --> 00:09:05,367
required video memory would erase most of

203
00:09:05,367 --> 00:09:07,050
the question we came here to answer.

204
00:09:07,370 --> 00:09:10,546
On eight gigabytes, regard the three-billion family as

205
00:09:10,546 --> 00:09:14,332
a constrained experiment candidate. Start with short examples,

206
00:09:14,332 --> 00:09:17,337
perhaps five hundred twelve tokens, a microbatch of

207
00:09:17,337 --> 00:09:20,474
one, and checkpointing. Those are starting choices,

208
00:09:20,474 --> 00:09:22,337
not a tested fit claim, so on

209
00:09:22,337 --> 00:09:24,930
twelve gigabytes retain that controlled setup first.

210
00:09:25,250 --> 00:09:27,550
Measure a complete training step: a forward

211
00:09:27,550 --> 00:09:30,194
pass, a backward pass, and an optimizer

212
00:09:30,194 --> 00:09:32,886
update. An optimizer can allocate state when

213
00:09:32,886 --> 00:09:35,398
the first update happens, so a forward-only

214
00:09:35,398 --> 00:09:37,820
demonstration can miss the failure that matters.

215
00:09:38,140 --> 00:09:40,525
At sixteen gigabytes, you have more freedom to

216
00:09:40,525 --> 00:09:43,866
test broader adapter targets or longer examples, one

217
00:09:43,866 --> 00:09:46,029
change at a time. At twenty-four, a

218
00:09:46,029 --> 00:09:49,503
seven-billion experiment becomes another candidate to investigate

219
00:09:49,503 --> 00:09:52,388
after the smaller control works. Neither capacity

220
00:09:52,388 --> 00:09:55,450
guarantees full fine-tuning or a long-context run.

221
00:09:55,770 --> 00:09:58,477
Also check the hardware behind the capacity. Our

222
00:09:58,477 --> 00:10:02,527
pinned normal-float configuration requires a compatible CUDA stack

223
00:10:02,527 --> 00:10:06,073
and a device supporting its selected brain-float computation.

224
00:10:06,073 --> 00:10:08,032
Two cards with the same memory label can

225
00:10:08,032 --> 00:10:11,390
require different precision choices and different software support.

226
00:10:11,710 --> 00:10:14,098
On an Apple machine with sixty-four or one

227
00:10:14,098 --> 00:10:17,451
hundred twenty-eight gigabytes of unified memory, the capacity

228
00:10:17,451 --> 00:10:20,143
number describes a different system. M L X

229
00:10:20,143 --> 00:10:22,643
L M has its own adapter training path

230
00:10:22,643 --> 00:10:25,204
for the Qwen two family. The CUDA configuration

231
00:10:25,204 --> 00:10:27,220
on screen is not a Mac recipe.

232
00:10:27,552 --> 00:10:30,857
For comparison, calculate this architecture's inference cache

233
00:10:30,857 --> 00:10:33,633
separately. One sequence at eight thousand one

234
00:10:33,633 --> 00:10:36,386
hundred ninety-two tokens needs two hundred eighty-eight

235
00:10:36,386 --> 00:10:39,258
mebibytes of logical key and value storage

236
00:10:39,258 --> 00:10:41,734
in sixteen-bit precision. That is about zero

237
00:10:41,734 --> 00:10:44,400
point three zero two decimal gigabytes.

238
00:10:44,720 --> 00:10:46,910
An ideal Q eight zero payload is

239
00:10:46,910 --> 00:10:50,347
one hundred fifty-three mebibytes, because each thirty-two-value

240
00:10:50,347 --> 00:10:53,773
block carries thirty-four bytes, including its scale.

241
00:10:53,773 --> 00:10:56,453
two hundred eighty-eight mebibytes needs two hundred

242
00:10:56,453 --> 00:11:00,455
eighty-eight mebibytes. Both figures exclude runtime overhead,

243
00:11:00,455 --> 00:11:02,070
and neither is a training peak.

244
00:11:02,390 --> 00:11:05,871
During a training step, autograd, loss computation,

245
00:11:05,871 --> 00:11:08,599
batch shape, and the attention implementation decide

246
00:11:08,599 --> 00:11:11,051
what must remain live on the device.

247
00:11:11,051 --> 00:11:12,710
Checkpointing has a time cost worth

248
00:11:12,710 --> 00:11:14,950
counting, because it recomputes the graph.

249
00:11:15,274 --> 00:11:17,714
Suppose the whole system averages two hundred fifty

250
00:11:17,714 --> 00:11:19,938
watts for one hour. That is zero point

251
00:11:19,938 --> 00:11:23,436
two five kilowatt-hours. A configuration that uses less

252
00:11:23,436 --> 00:11:25,671
memory but takes longer can consume more

253
00:11:25,671 --> 00:11:27,980
total energy, depending on its power draw.

254
00:11:28,301 --> 00:11:29,995
We have no measured power trace for

255
00:11:29,995 --> 00:11:32,796
this recipe. Log wall time and whole-system

256
00:11:32,796 --> 00:11:35,584
energy if efficiency matters to your decision.

257
00:11:35,584 --> 00:11:37,507
A smaller checkpoint file cannot tell you

258
00:11:37,507 --> 00:11:39,640
how many watt-hours its training consumed.

259
00:11:39,960 --> 00:11:41,893
The useful reason to train an adapter is

260
00:11:41,893 --> 00:11:44,405
a behavior you can describe and test.

261
00:11:44,405 --> 00:11:46,869
Here are three cases: write your team's

262
00:11:46,869 --> 00:11:50,402
issue summaries consistently, apply a stable classification

263
00:11:50,402 --> 00:11:53,430
policy, or follow a domain-specific response pattern.

264
00:11:53,750 --> 00:11:56,432
Build representative examples and keep a separate

265
00:11:56,432 --> 00:11:59,316
evaluation set. Compare the adapted result with

266
00:11:59,316 --> 00:12:01,444
the same base model, using the same

267
00:12:01,444 --> 00:12:04,160
prompt template and deployment precision. Check what

268
00:12:04,160 --> 00:12:06,792
improved and what regressed. If the underlying

269
00:12:06,792 --> 00:12:09,581
problem is simply a changing spreadsheet, first

270
00:12:09,581 --> 00:12:12,160
establish why changing the weights is necessary.

271
00:12:12,480 --> 00:12:15,355
The accompanying listing prints a configuration. It

272
00:12:15,355 --> 00:12:17,891
downloads no model and starts no training.

273
00:12:17,891 --> 00:12:20,115
A configuration is a proposal, and

274
00:12:20,115 --> 00:12:21,650
a proposal is not a run.

275
00:12:21,970 --> 00:12:24,609
The proposal is explicit. The revision, the

276
00:12:24,609 --> 00:12:27,553
target modules, the rank, the precision, the

277
00:12:27,553 --> 00:12:29,837
sequence length, and the microbatch are all

278
00:12:29,837 --> 00:12:31,990
named before any memory is allocated.

279
00:12:32,310 --> 00:12:34,168
A complete run needs more than the

280
00:12:34,168 --> 00:12:36,944
listing. It needs tokenized data, a checked

281
00:12:36,944 --> 00:12:40,117
loss mask, an evaluation procedure, and full-step

282
00:12:40,117 --> 00:12:41,932
profiling on the machine you actually

283
00:12:41,932 --> 00:12:44,030
own, not on a paper configuration.

284
00:12:44,350 --> 00:12:47,050
After training, two things must be preserved: the

285
00:12:47,050 --> 00:12:50,223
exact base revision and the adapter settings. Then

286
00:12:50,223 --> 00:12:53,095
check the deployed adapter too, because merging and

287
00:12:53,095 --> 00:12:56,809
quantized inference introduce another implementation choice that deserves

288
00:12:56,809 --> 00:12:59,090
its own validation on your own hardware.

289
00:12:59,410 --> 00:13:01,693
The verdict is simple. LoRA reduces what

290
00:13:01,693 --> 00:13:04,626
you update. Quantized LoRA also reduces how

291
00:13:04,626 --> 00:13:07,006
the frozen base is stored. Neither one

292
00:13:07,006 --> 00:13:09,170
removes the rest of the learning process.

293
00:13:09,490 --> 00:13:12,361
Buy capacity around a measured training workload,

294
00:13:12,361 --> 00:13:14,392
or reduce the workload until it matches

295
00:13:14,392 --> 00:13:16,844
the hardware you already own. The adapter

296
00:13:16,844 --> 00:13:19,104
is the change you keep. Training memory

297
00:13:19,104 --> 00:13:21,900
is the work required to discover it.
